Direct3D 9, being implemented.

The plan's Phase 3 is D3D9 on the pieces D3D11 already has: a D3D9 device over this build's D3D11 device, its chains
over DXGI's presenter, and draws through airconv, so a D3D9 game reaches Metal by the same road a D3D11 one does.

# How the source is organised
One owner per concern, so that a change to a draw's behaviour lands in one file rather than three:

  * d3d9_device.hpp and d3d9_device.cpp: the device object itself -- its interfaces, the Direct3D 11 device under it, the
    chain a frame presents through, the back buffer, and the CPU surfaces a frame is read back into.
  * d3d9_device_state.hpp and d3d9_device_state.cpp: the state a draw is made from, in one object -- the render states,
    the world, view and projection transforms with their composition, and the eight lights with their enables and the
    material. A Reset is that object's reset, and a state block captures and restores it through the device's calls. A
    draw asks it for what it needs (the composed matrix, the lit constants) instead of assembling it from members.
  * d3d9_fixed_function.hpp and d3d9_fixed_function.cpp: the fixed-function pipeline -- the shaders, input layouts and
    constant buffers of the formats this build transforms, and the binding of them for one `FixedFunctionDraw`
    description. The pipeline is the only code that touches those Direct3D 11 objects.
  * d3d9_device_draw.cpp: the draws -- validation (formats, ranges, caps, the alpha test this build cannot do), the
    primitives and indices, staging a `_UP` draw, and issuing the draw. It works out *what* a draw is (a
    `FixedFunctionDraw`, or a game's own programs) and the pipeline binds it; it does not choose a blob or map a buffer.
  * d3d9_device_targets.cpp: render targets, depth-stencil surfaces, the viewport and the scissor rectangle, and the
    device's render-state and transform entry points, which delegate to the state above.
  * d3d9_device_shader.cpp: a draw through a game's own shaders -- the vertex declaration, the translated programs and
    the constant tables they read.
  * d3d9_device_texture.cpp and d3d9_texture.cpp: the textures a draw samples and the stage that samples them;
    d3d9_device_transfer.cpp the copies between surfaces.
  * d3d9_state.cpp, d3d9_lights.cpp and d3d9_state_block.cpp: the parts of the state large enough to be their own
    translation unit. They are owned by `DeviceState` and the device interface, not by the device object.

The rule a later milestone follows: a new fixed-function format is a `FixedFunctionDraw` field and a blob, not a change
to the draw path; a new piece of state is a `DeviceState` member with a render state beside it, not a device member; a new
draw is validation plus one `prepare_draw()` call.

# Milestone 1 (done): the device and presentation
What a build with -Denable_d3d9=true makes (the option is off by default, so a contributor who does not ask for D3D9
gets the build they had):

  * Direct3DCreate9: the IDirect3D9 object over DXGI's adapters, which are this build's Metal devices. Adapter
    identity is DXGI's (the Metal device's name, vendor and device ids); the caps are the ones that adapter's D3D11
    device has, with the texture size of its feature level. The caps then named what the device of that milestone
    could do (a HAL that presented, and nothing else); each milestone below raised them to what its tests certify, and
    milestone 5c's test holds the shader versions and stage counts to the ones the device enforces.
  * CreateDevice with D3DDEVTYPE_HAL for a window: a D3D11 device over the same adapter, and the window's implicit
    swap chain through DXGI's factory (a flip model DISCARD chain, at least two buffers).
  * Clear of the back buffer, and Present: what was cleared is shown, four frames in a row
    (tests/d3d9/d3d9_present.cpp checks the fourth frame as it checks the first).
  * A surface read back: D3DPOOL_SYSTEMMEM surfaces are staging textures, and GetRenderTargetData copies the back
    buffer into one, which LockRect then reads.
  * The back buffer a chain hands out stays the same buffer across a Present. The chain turns its buffers over by
    giving buffer 0 the next buffer's storage (DXGI's flip model as this build implements it), so the view and the
    surface taken from it stay the frame's, and a game that keeps its surface draws into the frame being drawn.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_present.cpp is the test of this milestone, a window test since it presents to one. It reads each
frame back twice: out of the back buffer through GetRenderTargetData before Present, and out of the window through
GDI after it (the frame reaches the window through GDI because the test asks for dxgi.presentThroughGDI, since
nothing reads a Metal view's pixels back).

# Milestone 2 (done): geometry through the fixed-function path
What a build of this milestone draws, over the D3D11 device and draw path D3D11 already has:

  * IDirect3DVertexBuffer9 and IDirect3DIndexBuffer9: CreateVertexBuffer and CreateIndexBuffer (D3DPOOL_DEFAULT or
    D3DPOOL_MANAGED, D3DFMT_INDEX16/INDEX32), Lock and Unlock with D3D9's rules (one lock at a time; DISCARD and
    NOOVERWRITE only on a dynamic buffer and never both; a lock's range must fit the buffer), and GetDesc. The D3D11
    buffer under each is dynamic, which the D3D11 core only maps for a whole-buffer discard, so the wrapper keeps the
    buffer's bytes and Unlock uploads them: a lock that does not discard keeps what was there, as D3D9's does.
  * SetFVF with D3DFVF_XYZRHW | D3DFVF_DIFFUSE, a vertex already in screen space with a colour. Any other format is
    D3DERR_INVALIDCALL and the device keeps the format it had (tests/d3d9/d3d9_triangle.cpp checks that with a format
    that carries a normal). D3DFVF_XYZ, the vertex the device transforms, is milestone C's (below): the same pixel
    path, with the world, view and projection in front of it.
  * SetStreamSource (stream 0, an offset and a stride) and SetStreamSourceFreq (1, no instancing), SetIndices.
  * DrawPrimitive, DrawIndexedPrimitive and DrawPrimitiveUP, all three through shaders of the library's own (a vertex
    shader that maps the screen-space vertex through the viewport constant, and a pixel shader that writes the vertex
    colour) under the render state milestone 3 brings, which starts as D3DRENDERSTATETYPE's documented defaults: fill
    solid, cull counter-clockwise (so a screen-clockwise triangle is a front face and is drawn), depth test off until
    the device has an automatic depth-stencil surface or an application asks for it, source blend ONE and destination
    blend ZERO. The tests' own screen-space triangles are wound counter-clockwise, so those tests turn culling off.
  * What a draw refuses, with D3D9's code: a draw with no stream source, an indexed draw whose index range or vertex
    range does not fit the buffers it names, a base vertex that puts the range outside them, an FVF this milestone
    cannot transform, and a vertex buffer whose stride is not the format's. A draw of no primitives is S_OK and draws
    nothing.
  * The device holds the vertex buffer it is the stream source of: an application's Release does not take it away,
    and the draw after the release still draws.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_triangle.cpp draws four frames in a row, each a triangle of a known colour at a known place (a
vertex buffer, an index buffer, vertices in memory, and a vertex buffer the application released), and reads each
one back out of the back buffer and out of the window after Present. tests/d3d9/d3d9_draw_contract.cpp is what the
draw path refuses and what it keeps between calls.

# Milestone 3 (done): render states, depth-stencil surfaces and render targets
What a frame is drawn with and into, so that a basic frame is correct rather than merely visible:

  * SetRenderState and GetRenderState for the states the fixed-function path can honour: D3DRS_ZENABLE,
    ZWRITEENABLE, ZFUNC, CULLMODE, FILLMODE (D3DFILL_SOLID and D3DFILL_WIREFRAME; D3DFILL_POINT has no D3D11 fill
    mode and is refused as a value), SHADEMODE (D3DSHADE_GOURAUD and D3DSHADE_FLAT, which is its own pixel shader with
    a nointerpolation colour; D3DSHADE_PHONG is refused), D3DRS_ALPHABLENDENABLE, SRCBLEND, DESTBLEND, BLENDOP and
    COLORWRITEENABLE and SCISSORTESTENABLE. A state this build has no meaning for (fog, stencil state) is
    D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, said once, and a value that cannot be honoured is refused the same way; either leaves the
    device with the value it had, so a frame is never drawn under a state nobody asked for. The values a device
    starts with are the ones D3DRENDERSTATETYPE documents: CULLMODE D3DCULL_CCW, SRCBLEND D3DBLEND_ONE, DESTBLEND
    D3DBLEND_ZERO, ZWRITEENABLE TRUE, ZFUNC D3DCMP_LESSEQUAL, FILLMODE D3DFILL_SOLID, SHADEMODE D3DSHADE_GOURAUD,
    ALPHABLENDENABLE FALSE, BLENDOP D3DBLENDOP_ADD, COLORWRITEENABLE all four channels, TEXTUREFACTOR 0xFFFFFFFF, and
    ZENABLE TRUE only for a device whose chain made an automatic depth-stencil surface (FALSE otherwise).
  * CreateDepthStencilSurface (D3DFMT_D16, D24X8, D24X4S4, D24S8 and D32, D3DPOOL_DEFAULT), SetDepthStencilSurface and
    GetDepthStencilSurface. The surface has to be the target's size, and a target of another size than the depth
    surface is refused rather than bound half a pair. EnableAutoDepthStencil in the present parameters makes one of
    AutoDepthStencilFormat at device creation, so a game that asked for depth has it from its first frame.
  * Clear with D3DCLEAR_TARGET, D3DCLEAR_ZBUFFER and D3DCLEAR_STENCIL: the colour of the target being drawn into, and
    the depth and stencil of the surface set. A colour clear leaves depth as it was, and only D3DCLEAR_ZBUFFER resets it.
    The stencil is cleared with the depth, though no draw this milestone makes tests it yet. A clear of rects clears the
    colour and depth of each of them (clamped to the target, and a rect that names no pixel clears none), the depth
    through ID3D11DeviceContext1::ClearView, whose depth-stencil branch clears the depth of the rects it is given and
    nothing else (tests/d3d9/d3d9_targets.cpp draws a far triangle into one half of a cleared rect and a near one
    outside it, and reads back which of the two won where). A rect's stencil is left as it was, with the library saying
    so once: ClearView's depth-stencil branch clears depth alone, and a stencil-only clear of part of a view would need
    a draw that writes the stencil, which this path has no shader for.
  * ValidateDevice answers one pass for state it can draw with, and GetAvailableTextureMem answers what the Metal
    device recommends it may still take (IDXGIAdapter3::QueryVideoMemoryInfo: budget less what its textures have
    taken), which is the figure a title reads to size its textures.
  * CreateRenderTarget, SetRenderTarget (target 0; a target index but 0, a null surface and a depth surface are
    refused) and GetRenderTarget: Clear and the draws go to the target set, and the back buffer keeps what it had until
    SetRenderTarget names it again. A target brings its own viewport: setting one places the viewport on the whole of
    it (Wine's device_reset_viewport_state does the same), so a game that switches targets and draws without another
    SetViewport draws over the new target rather than over the old one's place in it. Present still presents the
    chain's back buffer, as D3D9's does.
  * SetViewport and GetViewport: the viewport places the vertex map (its origin and size) and is the draw's clip
    rectangle (D3D11's scissor), so a pixel outside it is not drawn whatever the triangle covers; a viewport of no size,
    outside the target, or with its depths outside 0 to 1 is refused and the device keeps the one it had.
  * SetScissorRect and GetScissorRect: while D3DRS_SCISSORTESTENABLE is on, every draw is clipped to the rectangle
    intersected with the viewport (one D3D11 scissor performs both clips). The rectangle cannot name a pixel outside
    the target (D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, the device keeping the one it had), an empty rectangle clips everything, and the
    device starts with the whole target. A new target is the whole of its own scissor until the application sets one.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_states.cpp is the state test: the defaults a device starts with, two triangles at different depths in
both drawing orders, depth off, depth writes off, ZFUNC, a colour clear against a depth clear, both cull windings with
both cull modes, wireframe against solid, blending under the default ONE/ZERO factors and SRCALPHA/INVSRCALPHA and
ONE/ONE, the colour write mask, GOURAUD against FLAT, and the states and values that are refused. It reports every state on its own: each SetRenderState of a state under test and each
pixel it is read out of is a check, and a state the device refuses is a `wrong:` line the run carries on past, so the
same test against milestone 2's device (which has no render state at all) named 36 wrongs across depth, cull, fill,
blend, colour write and shade instead of stopping at the first missing call. tests/d3d9/d3d9_targets.cpp is the
surfaces and viewport test: a custom target that takes the draw while the back buffer does not, the viewport it brings
and the one a draw is placed and clipped by, the depth surface on it, the automatic depth-stencil surface, and the
targets, depths and viewports that are refused.

The device's source is split by what a frame needs: d3d9_device.cpp has the object, its chain, its back buffer and
the surfaces a frame is read back into, d3d9_device_targets.cpp the render states, depth-stencil surfaces, render
targets and viewport, d3d9_device_draw.cpp the buffers and draws, d3d9_device_shader.cpp the pipeline a game's own
shaders draw through, d3d9_state_block.cpp the state blocks, d3d9_query.cpp the queries, and d3d9_shader_translator.cpp
the translation of their programs, over the class in d3d9_device.hpp.

# Milestone 3b (done): Reset
A windowed chain resized and reformatted again, which a game does when its window changes size or it returns from a
level:

  * Reset takes the BackBufferWidth, BackBufferHeight, BackBufferFormat, BackBufferCount, EnableAutoDepthStencil and
    AutoDepthStencilFormat of the parameters and resizes the implicit chain to them: D3DFMT_UNKNOWN keeps the format
    the chain had (the desktop's for a windowed chain), and a width or height of zero is the window's client area. The
    back buffer, the viewport and the swap chain's own present parameters are the new values.
  * the structure reports what D3D9's does when the call returns: BackBufferCount, BackBufferWidth and
    BackBufferHeight as zero and BackBufferFormat as D3DFMT_UNKNOWN, so a game that keeps it queries the chain again.
  * a reference the application still holds on the implicit back buffer or on a depth-stencil surface is
    D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, as D3D9's Reset requires those released; the chain is as it was. A multi-sampled chain, a swap
    effect other than D3DSWAPEFFECT_DISCARD and a format this build has none of are refused the same way, and are said
    once in the library's log. A fullscreen chain is not implemented: this build presents to a window.
  * all state is lost: the render states, the texture stages and samplers are the defaults, and the streams, indices,
    declaration, shaders set, textures and constants are unbound. The shader objects a game made survive, as D3D9's do,
    and a game sets its FVF, streams and shaders again before its next draw. A chain with EnableAutoDepthStencil gets a
    depth-stencil surface of the new size and depth testing on again.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_reset.cpp is the test: a live back buffer, a live depth surface and a fullscreen or multi-sampled chain
refused and the chain unchanged; a 0x0 UNKNOWN reset to the client area and the chain's format read back; the viewport,
swap chain parameters and automatic depth surface of the new size; the state the reset lost; and the clear that reads
back at the new size. tests/d3d9/d3d9_draw_contract.cpp draws after a Reset with its FVF and stream set again.

# Milestone A (done): the lost device and the road back
The cooperative level a device reports once the frames it drew failed, and the Reset that recovers it:

  * TestCooperativeLevel is D3DERR_DEVICENOTRESET once the Direct3D 11 device under this one was removed (a device that
    can be reset, which this build's can: Reset makes a new Direct3D 11 device), and S_OK while it is not.
  * the calls that draw or present answer D3DERR_DEVICELOST rather than drawing into a device that is gone: BeginScene,
    Clear, DrawPrimitive, DrawIndexedPrimitive, DrawPrimitiveUP, DrawIndexedPrimitiveUP and Present. EndScene still ends
    the scene it was in. A default-pool resource is refused with D3DERR_DEVICELOST as well, since the device that would
    own it is the one a Reset replaces; a managed texture is the CPU's and is made as it always is.
  * the removal is the Direct3D 11 device's: the first command buffer error of the queue is the reason the device keeps,
    and the event ID3D11Device4::RegisterDeviceRemovedEvent gave is signaled with it.
  * Reset is the road back. On a lost device it releases the failed Direct3D 11 device with everything the device held on
    it (the chain, the views, the constant and scratch buffers and the fixed-function state objects), makes a new device
    over the same adapter with a new chain over the same window, resets the state, and re-acquires the back buffer and
    the automatic depth-stencil surface. The present parameters are checked before any of that, so a Reset that names a
    window that is not one, a fullscreen chain, a multi-sampled chain or another swap effect is D3DERR_INVALIDCALL and
    leaves the device lost. Should the new device or its chain not be made, Reset answers D3DERR_DEVICENOTRESET and the
    device stays lost rather than reporting a recovery it did not make.
  * what a Reset keeps is the managed pool and its contents (IDirect3DDevice9::Reset: "D3DPOOL_MANAGED resources ... are
    not affected by a device reset"): a managed texture registers itself with its device, and a Reset puts its authored
    levels, compressed chains included, back onto the new device, so a draw after the Reset samples the pixels the game
    wrote before it. The default pool is not rebuilt: D3D9 has the application release it before a Reset, and a stale
    resource is not silently made again as something the application did not make.
  * the state a live Reset loses is lost here too, and the caller's present parameters report the fields D3DFMT_UNKNOWN
    and zero as D3D9's do.

Limits, stated rather than papered over: a surface taken from a managed texture with GetSurfaceLevel before a Reset still
addresses the texture the old device had (take it again after the Reset); an application's own default-pool render target
is not rebuilt; and the fullscreen and multi-sampled chains of a live Reset are still not implemented.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_lifecycle.cpp runs three times. Live: the level is S_OK and a frame draws and presents. Lost
(MULLION_D3D11_FAULT_DEVICE=1): every drawing call answers D3DERR_DEVICELOST and the level says D3DERR_DEVICENOTRESET.
--reset (MULLION_D3D11_FAULT_DEVICE=4): the whole sequence a title runs — a managed texture written on a live device, the
frames failing, a default-pool texture and a draw refused, a Reset naming the wrong window refused with the level
unchanged, the Reset that recovers, D3DERR_INVALIDCALL for the state it lost, and a frame drawn again that reads back the
managed texture's pixels and presents. No GPU fault is needed: the facility makes the run's Nth completed command buffer
a failed one, which is the state a fault leaves the device in (tests/d3d11/d3d11_device_removal.cpp is the Direct3D 11
contract itself).

# Milestone 4a (done): textures and the texture stage
What a draw samples: the textures the device holds for eight stages (MaxSimultaneousTextures is 8), the samplers of
those stages, and the one fixed-function blending stage that is stage 0's (MaxTextureBlendStages is 1, because the
textured pixel shader performs one stage's operation and no draw blends a second):

  * CreateTexture for D3DPOOL_MANAGED and D3DPOOL_DEFAULT textures of the formats d3d9_common.hpp names
    (D3DFMT_A8R8G8B8 and X8R8G8B8 and the handful around them, and the block-compressed DXT1 to DXT5 milestone 5b
    samples), with the levels asked for or (none asked for) the whole
    chain down to one texel, and GetLevelCount, GetLevelDesc and GetSurfaceLevel. A managed texture's levels are the
    CPU's: LockRect writes a level and UnlockRect uploads it, and the levels below level 0 are the ones the game
    authored until GenerateMipSubLevels asks the device to make them from level 0. Unlocking does not generate them:
    D3DPOOL_MANAGED is not D3DUSAGE_AUTOGENMIPMAP, and a read-only lock changes nothing. A level of a texture is a
    surface of the device, and a lock of that level through the texture or through the surface is the same lock
    (IMTLD3D9LockTarget). A default-pool texture is made with the chain its size has, whose levels UpdateTexture and
    a render-target draw fill (milestone 4c). A D3DUSAGE_RENDERTARGET texture is a default-pool texture with a render
    target view of each level, and a D3DUSAGE_DYNAMIC texture is one whose levels are the CPU's to stream into.
  * A texture's level desc is the texture's: its format, its pool (a managed texture's level is managed, a target's is
    default, a readback surface is system memory) and the size the chain halves down to.
  * SetTexture/GetTexture for stages 0 to 7 (the device holds what was set for each of them), SetSamplerState and
    GetSamplerState for those stages' samplers (a sampler is its stage's), and SetTextureStageState/GetTextureStageState
    for the fixed-function blender's one stage, stage 0's.
  * The stage's operations, as the textured pixel shaders' constants: D3DTOP_DISABLE, SELECTARG1, SELECTARG2, MODULATE,
    ADD and SUBTRACT for the colour op and the alpha op apart, over the arguments D3DTA_TEXTURE, D3DTA_DIFFUSE,
    D3DTA_CURRENT (the vertex colour at stage 0, which is the only stage there is) and D3DTA_TFACTOR, whose colour is
    D3DRS_TEXTUREFACTOR. An argument with a modifier (a complement or an alpha replication) is another argument and is
    refused, as is an operation, an argument or a texture-coordinate setting this build does not carry.
  * The sampler: the three filters (each pick is taken on its own whatever the other two are, and the draw samples
    through the closest D3D11 filter to the three -- see d3d9_texture.cpp), the address modes WRAP, MIRROR, CLAMP and
    MIRRORONCE for each of u, v and w, and MAXANISOTROPY from 1 to 16. A border address mode, a level-of-detail bias, a
    maximum mip level, a border colour, an sRGB texture and an element index are refused instead of sampled as
    something else (the states whose only usable value is this build's own -- a bias of none, the top level, a border
    nobody reads -- are taken).
  * What D3D9 says about a stage with no texture: a stage whose first colour argument is D3DTA_TEXTURE is not processed
    at all while no texture is bound, which for stage 0 is the vertex colour the frame already carries, and a texture
    argument in any other place samples a white texel (1, 1, 1, 1).
  * What a texture call refuses, with D3D9's code: a usage flag other than render target and dynamic (automatic
    mipmaps), a system-memory or scratch pool, a level count past the chain, a format this build has none of, a texture
    of no pixels, a fourth level of a size that has three, a lock of a texture whose bytes are not the CPU's, a second
    lock of the same texture, an unlock of a level that is not the locked one, and a stage or sampler the device has
    not.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_texture.cpp is the test of this milestone: an 8 by 8 texture written through its top level, whose four
quadrants are four texels of different colours (a pixel says which part of the texture it sampled) and whose last texel
is a colour of its own (so a pixel says the rows and columns arrived whole). It reads the frame back out of the back
buffer and, after Present, out of the window: the texels where their coordinates say, the vertex colour multiplying
and adding to them (MODULATE and ADD), each operation and argument, the texture's own alpha against the vertex's, a
wrapping sampler repeating the texture against a clamping one holding its edge, the anisotropic filter sampling the
same texels, and SHADEMODE (a FLAT draw takes one vertex's colour for the whole triangle, a GOURAUD one interpolates;
the test's triangle carries three colours so the two are not the same frame).

# Milestone 4c (done): the copies outside a draw
What a game makes of its surfaces between draws, each with D3D9's rules (tests/d3d9/d3d9_transfer.cpp):

  * CreateOffscreenPlainSurface for a system-memory surface (the CPU's, a readback's destination) and a default-pool one
    (the device's, a copy's source or destination and a ColorFill's target).
  * ColorFill of a default-pool surface, whole or in the rectangles named: a system-memory surface is refused, and a
    rectangle that names no pixel fills none.
  * StretchRect of the same size, with the source and destination rectangles, their offsets and the refusal of a copy
    that overlaps itself on one surface. A stretched copy, a format conversion and a system-memory destination are
    refused rather than copied as an unscaled one or the wrong format.
  * UpdateSurface: a system-memory rectangle written into a default-pool surface at a point, with no stretch and no
    conversion, through the row pitch the lock reports.
  * UpdateTexture: a managed texture's levels copied into a default-pool texture of the same format, size and level
    count, each level as the application authored it. A different level count, a default-pool source, a managed
    destination and one texture as both are refused.
  * A D3DUSAGE_RENDERTARGET texture: SetRenderTarget takes a level of it, a draw and a Clear reach that level at its own
    size (the view is the level's, not the top one's), and GetRenderTargetData reads the level back. A render-target
    texture in another pool, or one that is also dynamic, is refused.
  * A D3DUSAGE_DYNAMIC texture: LockRect with DISCARD (the level is scratch) or NOOVERWRITE (the pixels outside the
    rectangle are kept), UnlockRect uploading the level the application wrote, and READONLY refused as D3D9 refuses it
    for a dynamic texture. D3D11 maps a texture only with WRITE_DISCARD, so the level is authored on the CPU and
    uploaded, which is what keeps a NOOVERWRITE lock's other pixels.

# Milestone C-1 (done): the fixed-function transform
A D3DFVF_XYZ vertex is the one the device transforms: its position is in the space the world matrix reads, and the
device's world, view and projection take it to a clip position, which the rasterizer divides by w and maps through the
viewport (Microsoft Learn, "Transforms": v' = v * World * View * Projection; D3DVIEWPORT9).

  * SetTransform, GetTransform and MultiplyTransform for D3DTS_WORLD, D3DTS_VIEW and D3DTS_PROJECTION. They start
    identity, a Reset puts them back there, a null matrix is D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, and a transform this build does not
    read is refused rather than kept by nobody: D3D9's other 255 world matrices (which are vertex blending's, and this
    build blends no vertices) and the texture coordinate transforms (D3DTS_TEXTURE0 and up, which the one-stage
    texture blender does not perform). MultiplyTransform multiplies the matrix the device holds by the one given, the
    device's first, as D3D9's does. A state block of the vertex kind (and of all state) carries the three.
  * The draw composes world * view * projection when one of them changes rather than at every draw, and binds it at b0
    for the transform's own vertex shader, which multiplies the vertex by it: the divide by w, the viewport's
    rectangle and its MinZ..MaxZ range are the rasterizer's, from the viewport the draw binds, exactly as they are for
    a game's own vertex shader. The shaders are compiled offline from the source in
    tests/d3d9/fixed_function_shaders.cpp and carried as bytes in src/d3d9/d3d9_transform_shaders.hpp, so no shader is
    compiled at run time; the matrix is declared row_major, so the constant buffer holds what a D3DMATRIX holds.
  * D3DFVF_XYZ | D3DFVF_DIFFUSE and D3DFVF_XYZ | D3DFVF_DIFFUSE | D3DFVF_TEX1 draw through it, with the same colour and
    texture stage as the screen-space formats: the textured variant of the transform's shader carries the coordinates
    where the stage's pixel shader samples them.

What is not there yet, and is refused: the vertex blending whose world matrices are not kept, and the texture
coordinate transforms. tests/d3d9/d3d9_transform.cpp is the test:
an XYZ triangle under identity puts the same pixels on the frame as the equivalent XYZRHW one, every pixel compared; a
world translation of 0.5 moves the geometry exactly 16 pixels; a scale of 0.5 halves it about the origin; a rotation of
90 degrees turns it a quarter turn; a projection whose w is 2 halves the geometry's distance from the centre, which is
the divide and not merely a non-black frame; the viewport's 0.75..1.0 depth range keeps the nearer of two triangles in
front; and the transform state round-trips, composes in D3D9's order, survives a Reset and a state block.

# Milestone C-2 (done): fixed-function lighting
The lit vertex path, as D3D9's fixed function has it (Microsoft Learn, "Mathematics of Direct3D Lighting"):

  * D3DFVF_XYZ | D3DFVF_NORMAL | D3DFVF_DIFFUSE (and the same with one set of texture coordinates) draws as a
    transformed vertex does, and its colour is the device's D3DRS_AMBIENT plus the material's Emissive, plus for every
    enabled light the material's Ambient times the light's Ambient and, through the vertex's normal, the material's
    Diffuse times the light's Diffuse times max(0, n . L) times the light's attenuation and — while
    D3DRS_SPECULARENABLE is TRUE — the material's Specular times the light's Specular
    times max(0, n . H)^material.Power. H is the half vector between L and the eye, which is the world position the
    view matrix puts at its own origin. A directional light's L is the negation of its Direction (it points at the
    light); a point light's is its Position less the vertex, its attenuation is 1 / (A0 + A1 d + A2 d^2), and it
    contributes nothing past its Range.
  * SetLight, GetLight, LightEnable and GetLightEnable keep D3D9's eight lights (D3DCAPS9::MaxActiveLights) and an index
    past them is D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, as is a null light or output; SetMaterial and GetMaterial keep the one material, and
    the device's own starts as D3D9's does (diffuse white, the rest nothing), with every light disabled.
  * D3DRS_LIGHTING FALSE draws the vertex's own colour, D3DRS_NORMALIZENORMALS renormalises the transformed normal,
    and D3DRS_COLORVERTEX with D3DRS_AMBIENTMATERIALSOURCE or D3DRS_DIFFUSEMATERIALSOURCE set to D3DMCS_COLOR1 takes
    those two reflectances from the vertex's diffuse colour (D3DMCS_COLOR2 needs a vertex specular colour, which the
    formats this build draws do not carry).
  * the normal is turned by the inverse transpose of the world matrix (the upper 3 by 3, with the identity standing in
    when the world has no inverse), and the lighting is computed per vertex, as D3D9's fixed function does: the
    rasterizer interpolates the four vertices' colours.
  * the whole of it — the material, the eight lights with their enables, the global ambient, the eye, the specular power
    and the state flags — is one constant buffer at b1 (src/d3d9/d3d9_lights.hpp's LightConstants), written once per
    lit draw; the vertex shader that reads it is compiled offline from the source in tests/d3d9/fixed_function_shaders.cpp
    with the transform's, and carried as bytes in src/d3d9/d3d9_lighting_shaders.hpp. Every field is a float4 or a
    float4x4 so that the C structure and the shader's cbuffer agree without packing rules between them. The eight lights
    are spelled out one expansion each rather than looped over, because the HLSL compiler the blobs are generated with
    implements no loop unrolling and a constant buffer read has to be at a constant offset: the loop form does not
    compile at all, so the header can only be regenerated from the expanded source (the commands are at the top of the
    generator). A light that is off is skipped rather than multiplied by zero: the seven lights a device has and a game
    never set have no attenuation and no Range, and a term of theirs can be infinite, which times an `enabled` of zero
    is a NaN that poisons the sum of the enabled ones -- the frame then lights the face at one edge and fades it across
    the rest, which is what tests/d3d9/d3d9_lighting.cpp's full-face check reads.
  * a vertex-state block (and an all-state block) carries the eight lights, which of them is enabled, and the material,
    as D3D9's does; a recorded one records SetLight, LightEnable and SetMaterial as they are called.

The lights a lit draw reads are D3DLIGHT_POINT and D3DLIGHT_DIRECTIONAL. A D3DLIGHT_SPOT is kept and round-trips, and
the shader carries its cone (Theta, Phi and Falloff), but no check yet proves a spot light's intensity and neither
D3DRS_FOG* nor multiple texture stages are lit by this stage. tests/d3d9/d3d9_lighting.cpp is the test: the state D3D9
starts with; lighting off drawing the vertex's colour; the global ambient alone, a directional light's diffuse alone, and
the two added; a light pointing away contributing nothing; a light turned 90 degrees lighting the face whose normal
points at it and not the other; a point light's attenuation at d = 1 and d = 2 with Attenuation1, with Attenuation2 alone
and with Attenuation0 alone, and its Range cutting it off; the material's emissive; the specular term only while
D3DRS_SPECULARENABLE is TRUE; LightEnable and GetLightEnable; two lights adding; D3DRS_COLORVERTEX taking the diffuse from
the vertex; the four calls' round-trips, null pointers and an index past MaxActiveLights; and a vertex-state block
restoring the light, its enable and the material.

# Milestone 4b: the rest of what a game draws with
The rest of the fixed-function pipeline and the resources around it, still over D3D11: the texture coordinate formats,
the remaining texture features a game asks for (a second and further stages, cube and volume textures, D3DXFill-style
level writing; dynamic and render-target textures are 4c's) and the rest of the render states (fog, stencil
operations, point fill). The world, view and projection transform is C-1's and the lit vertex path is C-2's, above.
Vertex declarations are milestone 5a's, DrawIndexedPrimitiveUP, state blocks and event queries are 5c's, and
the alpha test is 5c's for a game's own pixel shader (the fixed-function path still has none).

# Milestone 5a (done): the draw a game's own shaders make
CreateVertexShader and CreatePixelShader take a Direct3D 9 program (a d3dbc stream whose version token is read and
whose instructions are read to D3DSIO_END; a stream that is not one is D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, as D3D9's is) of the versions
the caps name: a vertex shader of 1.1 or 2.0 and a pixel shader of 2.0, and a program of a higher model is refused where
it is made (milestone 5c's caps test holds the two together). GetFunction hands the tokens back. The draw is the game's:
both stages are its programs, its SetVertexDeclaration says what a vertex is, and its float, integer and boolean
constant tables reach the shaders.

What the translation (d3d9_shader_translator.cpp) writes is the shader model 5 the D3D11 device this build already has
compiles (airconv takes DXBC), so a D3D9 game's shaders reach Metal by the road a D3D11 game's do:

  * the register files the two stages link through, because a compiled program links by register and not by name: o0 is
    the position (a system value, declared with D3D10_SB_OPCODE_DCL_OUTPUT_SIV, which is what a Metal vertex function's
    [[position]] comes from), o1 and o2 are the two colours, and o3 to o7 are the texture coordinates. A pixel shader's
    v0 and v1 are o1 and o2 and its t# is o3 + t#, so a vertex shader writes its outputs where the pixel shader it
    draws with reads them. Direct3D 11 gives a vertex shader eight output registers, so a program that needs more than
    the five texture coordinates this leaves is refused rather than written over another output.
  * a vertex declaration element is an input register: the vertex shader's input signature declares exactly the
    components its element carries and its layout is the declaration, and the components a vertex does not carry read as
    D3D9 says they do (w is 1, the rest 0), which the translation completes in a temporary the program reads instead
    (`mov r1.xyz, v0.xyzx` then `mov r1.w, l(1.000000)`). A vertex shader is translated against the pixel shader it
    draws with and the declaration the draw reads, and the pair is kept by the shader objects: the draw after it costs
    a lookup.
  * the constant tables are the three constant buffers a draw binds (256 float4 registers as CB0, 16 int4 as CB1, 16
    booleans as CB2), and DEF, DEFI and DEFB are the immediates they define.
  * the instructions a game's shaders are made of: MOV, ADD, SUB, MUL, MAD, MIN, MAX, DP3, DP4, FRC, RCP, RSQ, EXP, LOG,
    ABS, SLT, SGE, LRP, POW, NRM, the texture sample TEX (milestone 5b) and the matrix multiplies M4x4, M4x3, M3x4,
    M3x3 and M3x2, with all four component masks, swizzles and the negate, absolute and absolute-negate modifiers, on
    temporaries, constants, inputs and outputs.
  * what a draw refuses rather than draws as something else: the texture instructions of shader model 1.x (the texture
    registers TEXBEM, TEXBEML, TEXDP3TEX, TEXM3x2TEX and TEXM3x3TEX sample), TEXLDD and TEXLDL, a vertex shader's own
    TEX, relative addressing of the constant tables (c# with an address register, a0), shader model 3's flow control
    (IF, IFC, LOOP, REP, CALL and their ends), the fog and point-size outputs, and a register the eight output registers
    cannot place. The reason is in the library's log, once, and the draw answers D3DERR_INVALIDCALL.

A translated program can be read back: `MULLION_DXBC_DUMP` names a directory, and every translation is written there
as `vs1.bin`, `ps1.bin` and so on, which Microsoft's disassembler (d3dcompiler_47) reads as the program it is.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_shader_draw.cpp is the test of this milestone: a triangle whose vertex colour a hand-written vs_2_0 and
ps_2_0 draw (the tokens are the test's, so the translator is proved without a compiler) and the same two stages as
Microsoft's compiler makes of HLSL at test time, each read back out of the back buffer: the colour where the triangle
covers, the clear where it does not, a zero pixel constant making it black, and a zero vertex constant collapsing it. A
program that samples a sampler this device does not have is refused with D3DERR_INVALIDCALL rather than drawn.
tests/d3d9/d3d9_shader.cpp is the refusals: a stream that is not a program, a program of another model, a shader with no
declaration to read.

# Milestone 5b (done): a game's shader samples a texture
What a pixel shader's texture sample is, end to end: the program's `dcl_2d s0` and `texld r#, t#, s0` (shader model 2
and 3's texture instruction, which names its sampler) are translated to shader model 5's `dcl_sampler s0`,
`dcl_resource_texture2d t0` and `sample`, and the draw binds what they read:

  * the texture `SetTexture(0)` holds is the resource, and the sampler the device's sampler states make
    (D3DSAMPLERSTATETYPE: the three filters, the address modes and MAXANISOTROPY, as milestone 4a's sampler already
    built them) is the sampler. Both are bound to the pixel shader's registers 0 and only when the program samples, so a
    program that does not never makes a render target it draws into a shader input too.
  * a coordinate is read as a 2D sample does: the sampler's first two components, on the texel grid of the level the
    filter picks. The texel's four components are its format's, sampled as floats (A8R8G8B8's alpha included).
  * a stage with no texture samples the default texture, one opaque white texel, which is what D3D9's default texture
    is: the layer makes one and binds it, so a sampling draw with nothing set is a white texel rather than an
    undefined one.
  * a program may sample any of the eight stages: `dcl_2d s#` and `texld r#, t#, s#` are that stage's sampler and
    texture, and a draw binds all eight stages' textures and samplers (each stage's, or the default white texel for a
    stage with none). A sampler past s7 is refused, as D3D9 refuses a stage the device does not have. Cube and volume
    samplers (dcl_cube, dcl_volume, dcl_3d) are refused too, as are TEXLDD, TEXLDL, a vertex shader's TEX (vertex
    texture fetch) and the texture instructions of shader model 1.x, each with the reason in the library's log once.
  * a compressed texture is the blocks it is stored as: D3DFMT_DXT1, DXT2 and DXT3, DXT4 and DXT5 are D3D11's BC1, BC2
    and BC3, a level's pitch is its blocks' rows (8 or 16 bytes a block), a lock is of whole four-by-four blocks (D3D9
    refuses a rectangle that starts inside one), and a texture of a size that is not whole blocks is refused. Each level
    of a compressed texture is the one the game uploads: the chain is made whole and no level is generated out of
    another (D3D11 makes none of a compressed texture), which is what a title that uploads a DXT chain per level does.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_texture_draw.cpp is the test: a 4 by 4 texture whose four columns are four colours is drawn over the
whole frame by a hand-written vs_2_0 and ps_2_0 (the tokens are Microsoft's compiler's own for the same program) and by
that program as Microsoft's compiler makes it of HLSL at test time, and read back out of the back buffer: the four
columns under the point filter, the blend of two texels under the linear one at the pixel between their centres (which
the point filter draws as one of them), a coordinate outside the texture under the clamp mode holding the edge texel
against the wrap mode repeating it, a DXT1 texture's block drawing its colour0, and an empty stage drawing white.

# Milestone 5c (done): the pixels a program kills, and the calls a title makes before it draws
The alpha test a title draws its sprites with, and the startup calls a title blocks on:

  * texkill is the discard of a pixel whose register has a negative component: the four components are compared with
    zero, joined, and the pixel discarded, wherever the instruction sits in the program (D3D9 kills the pixel rather
    than ordering the writes around it, which is what a discard in shader model 5 does too). It reads the register the
    instruction names, so a pixel shader that kills on one of its inputs declares that input; `dcl t0; texkill t0` is
    the shape Microsoft's compiler makes of `clip(uv.x)`.
  * D3DRS_ALPHATESTENABLE, D3DRS_ALPHAFUNC and D3DRS_ALPHAREF are the alpha test on the alpha a game's pixel shader
    writes. Direct3D 11 has no alpha test of its own, so a pixel shader translated for a draw under the test ends with
    its comparison against the reference and a discard when it fails: the program's first target is written to a copy
    the test reads, D3DRS_ALPHAREF reaches it as register b3 (so the same program is drawn with the test and without
    it), and a test of D3DCMP_ALWAYS passes everything, so it is the program without one. The translation is kept per
    alpha test a draw has used the program with.
  * a fixed-function draw under D3DRS_ALPHATESTENABLE with an ALPHAFUNC that can fail is D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, said
    once: the fixed-function pixel shaders this build carries have no alpha test, and a caption that should be cut out
    is not drawn as a quad instead. A game's own pixel shader has one, which is the path a title draws its sprites with.
  * DrawIndexedPrimitiveUP: the vertex and index arrays in memory are staged into the buffers the draw reads (16- and
    32-bit indices) and the primitives the indices name are drawn through the same path as every other draw; a
    primitive with no index array, or an index format that is neither, is refused.
  * a D3DQUERYTYPE_EVENT query: GetData answers S_FALSE while the GPU is still working on what was issued before it
    and S_OK with a nonzero flag once it is done, which is the wait a title paces a frame with. An unissued query has
    nothing to wait for, D3DISSUE_BEGIN is refused (an event query ends where it is issued), and an occlusion,
    timestamp or vertex-cache query is refused rather than answered with a number nothing measured.
  * CreateStateBlock of D3DSBT_ALL, D3DSBT_PIXELSTATE or D3DSBT_VERTEXSTATE, with Capture, Apply and GetDevice, and
    BeginStateBlock/EndStateBlock's recording mode: a block carries the values the device holds for the kind of state
    it names, Capture reads them again, Apply writes them back through the device's own calls, and a block recorded
    between BeginStateBlock and EndStateBlock carries only the states those calls set. A state the device refuses is
    refused to the block too.
  * the caps name the shader models this build certifies: VertexShaderVersion and PixelShaderVersion say vs_2_0 and
    ps_2_0, MaxVertexShaderConst 256 registers, the flow-control depths 0, MaxSimultaneousTextures 8 and
    MaxTextureBlendStages 1. CreateVertexShader and CreatePixelShader make a program of those versions and refuse one
    above them, so the numbers and the device agree. tests/d3d9/d3d9_caps.cpp holds them to it: the numbers on the
    adapter's caps and the device's, a vs_2_0/ps_2_0 pair drawn and its pixel read back, a vs_3_0 and a ps_3_0 refused
    where they are made, a ps_1_1 refused too (its registers are not translated yet), and the last stage the caps
    count accepted while the next one is refused.

tests/d3d9/d3d9_device_calls.cpp is the test of the startup calls: DrawIndexedPrimitiveUP drawing a quad with 16- and
32-bit indices, an event query waited on until it signals, and a state block captured, changed, applied, and the frame
after it drawn with the restored state. tests/d3d9/d3d9_discard.cpp is the test of the discards: a program that kills on
a negative coordinate, and a program drawn with and without the alpha test, each read back to show which half of a quad
survived.

# What is not there yet, and is refused rather than pretended
  * anything that makes a resource this build does not have (cube and volume textures, scratch-pool textures): E_NOTIMPL
    or D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, with the library saying so once. `SetVertexShader(NULL)` and
    `SetPixelShader(NULL)` answer S_OK, as D3D9 does: they choose the fixed-function pipeline, as one of the two stages
    being set chooses the program one
  * a vertex format other than D3DFVF_XYZRHW or D3DFVF_XYZ with D3DFVF_DIFFUSE, with or without D3DFVF_NORMAL and one
    set of texture coordinates: D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, and the device keeps the format it had, so a frame is not drawn
    with a transform nobody asked for
  * a transform other than the world, view and projection matrices: D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, said once (the vertex blending
    world matrices and the texture coordinate transforms)
  * a render state this milestone has no meaning for, and a value it cannot honour (D3DFILL_POINT, D3DSHADE_PHONG,
    BOTHSRCALPHA, BOTHINVSRCALPHA and the D3DRS_BLENDFACTOR factors): D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, with the device keeping what it had
  * SetLOD beyond the top level: the call answers with the level the texture is on (D3D9's SetLOD answers with the one
    it had) and says once, in the library's log, that the most detailed level is the top one -- the levels below it are
    the game's or made explicitly, and a texture is sampled from level 0 whatever it asks for. GetAutoGenFilterType
    answers D3DTEXF_LINEAR and SetAutoGenFilterType is D3DERR_INVALIDCALL: the automatic mip filter is a
    D3DUSAGE_AUTOGENMIPMAP texture's, and this build makes a managed texture's levels only when GenerateMipSubLevels
    asks it to and the format supports D3D11's generator
  * a render target or a depth-stencil surface with more than one sample, and a render target the CPU locks:
    D3DERR_NOTAVAILABLE or D3DERR_INVALIDCALL rather than a target that is not what it says
  * clearing the stencil of part of a surface (its colour and depth are cleared, and its stencil is left as it was,
    said once: this build's ClearView clears depth alone, and a stencil-only clear of part of a view needs a draw that
    writes the stencil)
  * a fixed-function draw under D3DRS_ALPHATESTENABLE with an ALPHAFUNC that can fail: D3DERR_INVALIDCALL, said once
    (the fixed-function pixel shaders this build carries have no alpha test; a draw through a game's own pixel shader
    carries it)
  * the second and further fixed-function blending stages (their operations) and lights: D3DERR_INVALIDCALL (a game's
    own shader may sample all eight stages, which is what the caps' MaxSimultaneousTextures says); an occlusion,
    timestamp or vertex-cache query: D3DERR_INVALIDCALL rather than a number nothing measured
  * a fullscreen chain, additional swap chains, GetFrontBufferData and CreateAdditionalSwapChain: not implemented
    (a windowed Reset is milestone 3b's)

# Seeing what a game asked for
Every call this layer refuses says so once, in the library's log, with what it was asked for: the format, the shader
version, the state, and the call itself. A game that stops at one of them names what to implement next instead of
leaving its HRESULT to guess, which is how this layer is grown against real titles.

A build writes that log where `MULLION_LOG_PATH` names a directory, as `<the game's executable>_mullion.log`; without
it the lines go to Wine's console. `MULLION_LOG_LEVEL` chooses how much is kept ("info" by default, "warn" for the
refusals alone). Both are the environment's, so a bottle's environment is where they are set for a title.
